{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-36488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-8)-9\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-36488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3518c59e8a7bd6882ef7ebc931ea4a96d090ca19549fdd373a20c50d133edab4"} {"question" "q-36488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-36488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-36488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-37154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-37154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-37154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3518c59e8a7bd6882ef7ebc931ea4a96d090ca19549fdd373a20c50d133edab4"} {"question" "q-37154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-37154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-37154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3518c59e8a7bd6882ef7ebc931ea4a96d090ca19549fdd373a20c50d133edab4"} {"question" "q-82614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3518c59e8a7bd6882ef7ebc931ea4a96d090ca19549fdd373a20c50d133edab4"} {"question" "q-82707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3518c59e8a7bd6882ef7ebc931ea4a96d090ca19549fdd373a20c50d133edab4"} {"question" "q-82718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077665/state.json" "file_sha256" "e98322f290d961f8c21bef57e50dfc71bc23b63b83a5c3578b6c6c7a3bd19f4a"} {"question" "q-82718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -9\\end{